00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  are you ready for the uk transition  number three continuing to trade  hello my name is jane rose and i'm head  of international trade at the essex  chambers of commerce  this is a third of four bite-sized  videos about the uk transition  which have been commissioned by the  rochford district council to help  businesses prepare for the changes  to trading with the eu in this video  i'm going to talk about how to help you  continue to export an import with the eu  next year  we want to help you get ready if this is  new to you  it can be daunting but don't worry share  the burden with your colleagues  contact and website details are listed  in the video description  so what do you need to do now to prepare  to continue trading with the eu from  january 2021  the first thing you'll need to do is  check that you have a uk  iori number which stands for economic  operators registration and  identification number if you have it  will start with a gb  and include your vat number if not then  apply now  it's quick and easy to do anything you  import or export to the eu will require  a customs declaration from the 1st of  january  for imports you may be able to delay the  full import declaration for six months  but you'll need to find out if your  goods qualify first  and remember you'll have to make the  full declaration by first of july next  year  if your goods qualify and you do want to  delay the declaration  you or your agent broker will need to  look into opening a duty deferment  account  as mentioned in my previous video you'll  need to complete the form  c88 or sad a single administrative  document  to make customs declarations you should  think carefully about who will make  these declarations  to do it yourself you'll need training  specialist software  and the import badge for the relevant  port of entry  you can appoint somebody to make the  declaration for you  a customs broker an agent or a forwarder  but either way there is time and cost  involved  the british chamber of commerce network  do offer a custom service  so please get in touch if you'd like to  know more  if you do decide to use a third party to  make declarations  it's important to check that the  information you give to them is correct  you're responsible not them you'll need  to know the commodity codes to classify  and value the goods that you're  importing or exporting  this code will give you information on  duties  vat licence requirements quotas and more  you can find these codes on the uk  global tariff for imports and the  exporters trade tool  the links are on our website you'll also  need to look at how you are going to  handle vat  you may want to use postponed accounting  there are custom special procedures  which can reduce  the duty you pay in some circumstances  so i would look into how this could  benefit you  you'll need to keep records for four  years even if somebody else makes the  declaration for you  it's really important in case you have  future customs audits  speak with your logistics provider too  as there are changes and regulations  that they need to be aware of such as  international driving permits  and the new goods vehicle movement  system  the government border operating model  has more information  on all of these topics that was a very  basic run-through  of some of the things that you'll need  to think about but don't be put off  start to prepare now and remember there  is help available  you can contact us at the essex chambers  of commerce if you need some guidance or  training  contact and website details are listed  in the video description  thank you so much for listening  [Music]

